.TH SUMTAG 1 "June 2026" "sumtag 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sumtag \- stamp files with an XXH3 checksum and metadata for corruption and duplicate detection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sumtag
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I DIRECTORY
.RI [ DIRECTORY .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sumtag
recursively scans each
.IR DIRECTORY ,
computes an XXH3 checksum of every file's contents, and stores the checksum
together with metadata in an extended attribute named
.B user.sumtag
on the file. The recorded metadata makes it possible to later detect silent
data corruption and to find duplicate files. Those higher-level analyses are
left to other tools, with one exception built in here: the single-pass
integrity check offered by
.BR \-\-verify .
.PP
An explicit action option is required: every run must name what it does with
one of
.BR \-\-sum ", " \-\-verify ", " \-\-remove ", " \-\-import ", " \-\-locate ", or " \-\-prune\-dirs .
A bare
.B sumtag
.I DIRECTORY
is an error. The plain stamping action is
.BR \-\-sum ,
which works with or without a database (see OPTIONS).
.PP
At least one
.I DIRECTORY
is also required; there is no current-directory default. Any operation that
scans a directory requires an explicit directory argument, so that a recursive
operation is never aimed at the working directory by omission. Pass
.B .
explicitly to scan the current directory.
.PP
For each file, sumtag reads any existing
.B user.sumtag
attribute and compares the recorded modification time to the file's current
mtime. If they match, the file is considered up to date and is skipped.
Otherwise the file's contents are hashed and the attribute is written.
.PP
Within each directory, files are processed in ascending alphabetical order
and subdirectories are then recursed into in the same order, so the
processing order can be followed against an
.BR ls (1)
listing. This holds in every mode.
.PP
Symbolic links are not content: a symlink encountered during traversal is
never followed, hashed, stamped, verified, or removed, in any mode. A
.I DIRECTORY
named on the command line may itself be a symbolic link, which is honoured;
nothing found beneath it is followed.
.SH RE-HASHING
A file is hashed, or re-hashed, when any of the following hold:
.IP \(bu 3
the
.B \-\-force
option was given;
.IP \(bu 3
the
.B user.sumtag
attribute is absent or unreadable;
.IP \(bu 3
the stored modification time is older than the file's current mtime;
.IP \(bu 3
the stored schema version has a lower major number than the running software.
.PP
Freshness is algorithm-agnostic: a file with any current digest, regardless of
which algorithm produced it, counts as up to date. A stored digest is never
recomputed merely because a different algorithm is now preferred; when a
re-hash does happen for one of the reasons above, it replaces the file's
single stored digest rather than keeping the old one alongside it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Scan and report what would be done, but write nothing \(em no extended
attributes and no database rows. Output matches a real run. Cannot be combined
with
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress normal output. Repeat
.RB ( \-qq )
to also suppress error messages on standard error. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Explain the decision made for each file, including skips. Without this
option, each file touched is announced by its bare path alone. Repeat
.RB ( \-vv )
for deep internal detail intended for debugging. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-quiet .
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Show a live within-file progress indicator for large files. If it appears
together with
.BR \-q ,
whichever option appears last on the command line wins, and a warning is
printed to standard error. The indicator's line shows the file's size, the
current hashing rate, a bar, the percentage complete, elapsed time, and an
ETA, e.g.:
.RS
.EX
99.3GiB   40.1MiB/s  [===============>     ]  84%   0:05:12  ETA 58s
.EE
.RE
.IP
The bar is the only field whose width varies: it absorbs whatever room the
fixed-width fields leave, and resizes live when the terminal window is
resized.
.TP
.B \-\-si
Display
.B \-\-progress
sizes and rates using decimal (SI, powers-of-1000:
.BR kB / MB / GB )
units instead of the default binary (powers-of-1024:
.BR KiB / MiB / GiB )
units.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Re-hash every file unconditionally, ignoring any existing metadata. Cannot be
combined with
.BR \-\-dry\-run " or " \-\-verify .
Combined with
.BR \-\-import " or " \-\-locate ,
overrides their default refusal to compute (see below). This option does
.I not
override
.B @sumtag\-ignore
markers; use
.B \-\-no\-ignore
for that.
.TP
.BI \-\-database " VALUE"
Name a database to act on.
.I VALUE
is a SQLite file path, or a
.RI scheme ://
DSN. A value matching the pattern
.RB ` ^[a-z][a-z0-9+.\-]*:// '
is treated as a DSN
.RB ( mysql:// ", " postgresql:// );
any other value, including one that merely contains a colon, is a SQLite file
path. Only SQLite is implemented; a DSN is recognised and rejected as not yet
supported. Database paths are stored relative to the file's mount point so
they survive remounting at a different location.
.B \-\-database
takes no action by itself; it requires at least one of
.BR \-\-sum ", " \-\-import ", " \-\-locate ", or " \-\-prune\-dirs
to say what to do with it.
.TP
.B \-\-sum
The stamping action: hash or re-hash each file per the normal mtime-based
decision (see
.B RE\-HASHING )
and write the extended attribute. With
.BR \-\-database ,
the result is additionally mirrored into the database; without one, only the
extended attribute is written.
.TP
.B \-\-import
Do not compute any checksum by default; only copy metadata already present in
each file's extended attribute into the database. Requires
.BR \-\-database .
Files lacking usable metadata are skipped and reported. Useful for populating
a database from an archive that was hashed on a previous run. Combined with
.BR \-\-force ,
computes and mirrors every file instead of only propagating what already
exists. Combined with
.BR \-\-sum ,
is redundant (the normal decision already computes and mirrors) but not an
error. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-verify .
.TP
.B \-\-locate
Stat every file visited and write filesystem metadata (size, permissions,
ownership, link count, device, and timestamps) to the database, regardless of
whether xattr work was done. Implies
.BR \-\-import :
existing extended-attribute metadata is propagated as well, so
.B \-\-locate
alone does everything
.B \-\-import
alone would, plus stat columns. Requires
.BR \-\-database .
Combined with
.BR \-\-force ,
computes checksums for every file too, the same override
.B \-\-import
accepts. Useful as a periodic filesystem inventory pass (analogous to
.BR updatedb ).
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Read-only integrity check: recompute each file's checksum and compare it to the
stored value, reporting any disagreement. Nothing is written, even on a
mismatch. A mismatch while the stored modification time still matches the
file's mtime indicates silent corruption; a mismatch accompanied by a newer
mtime indicates an ordinary modification whose stamp is merely stale. Files
with no usable metadata are reported as unverifiable. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-database ", " \-\-sum ", " \-\-import ", " \-\-locate ", or " \-\-force ";"
combining with
.B \-\-dry\-run
is a redundant no-op and is permitted. See
.B EXIT STATUS
below.
.TP
.B \-\-remove
Remove the
.B user.sumtag
xattr from every file in the tree. A testing/reset utility, not a
data-integrity primitive: no mtime comparison, no hashing, just an
unconditional delete of whatever attribute happens to be present. Files with
no attribute are left alone. Combined with
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
previews which files would lose their stamp without removing anything. Cannot
be combined with
.BR \-\-database ", " \-\-sum ", " \-\-import ", " \-\-locate ", " \-\-verify ", or " \-\-force .
.TP
.B \-\-prescan
Walk the tree once up front to count the files that will be checksummed and
their total size, then prefix each hash/verify announcement with an
.IR nnn / mmm
file counter and a bytes-so-far/total counter, e.g.:
.RS
.EX
042/137  118.2MiB/4.2GiB  hash /backup/vault/photo0042.dng (reason)
.EE
.RE
The counted set mirrors whichever mode is running: for the normal hash/stamp
pass it is the files the re-hash decision (or
.BR \-\-force )
will actually hash; for
.BR \-\-verify
it is every file with a usable stored digest. During the counting walk each
directory visited is announced before any file metadata inside it is read
(the bare directory path, or
.I prescan path
with
.BR \-v ;
suppressed by
.BR \-q ),
so the otherwise-silent up-front pass shows its own progress on a large
tree. The prescan pass is a
prediction from a separate walk, not a guarantee \(em counts can drift if the
tree changes between the two passes. On a
.B \-\-database
run (and not under
.BR \-n ),
the counted totals are also stored in the database as its single prescan
summary, replacing any previous one; that summary is what
.B \-\-db\-prescan
later consumes. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-remove .
.TP
.B \-\-db\-prescan
Like
.BR \-\-prescan ,
but load the counters' totals from the summary a previous
.B \-\-prescan \-\-database
run stored, instead of walking the filesystem \(em on a very large tree this
replaces an hours-long counting walk with a database read. The stored totals
are used only if they were counted for the same scan roots (compared after
path normalization) and under the same counting options
.RB ( \-\-sum / \-\-import / \-\-locate ", " \-\-force ", " \-\-exclude ", " \-\-no\-ignore );
a missing or non-matching summary is an error before any work begins (exit
status 2), never a silent fall-back to a filesystem walk. What was loaded is
announced at startup. The progress report is an approximation by consent: the
per-file counter tracks what actually happens this run, so it may overshoot
the stored total (the tree changed) or the run may finish below it (some of
the counted work was already done \(em the typical case when resuming an
interrupted scan). The stored data is display-only and never influences which
files are hashed. Requires
.BR \-\-database ;
cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-prescan " or " \-\-remove .
.TP
.B \-\-prune\-dirs
Reconcile the database with a changed filesystem: collect every directory the
database knows under the given roots (the distinct parent directories of its
file rows), check each one with a single
.BR stat (2),
and delete the rows of directories that no longer exist. A deleted directory
takes exactly its resident file rows with it; nothing is deleted recursively,
because a vanished subdirectory is discovered by its own check \(em every
known directory is in the list. The filesystem is never modified. Each
.I DIRECTORY
named on the command line must exist \(em a missing root is an error (exit
status 2) before the database is even opened, so an unmounted drive can never
read as a mass deletion \(em and only rows recorded under the root's current
mount point are candidates. The
.I DIRECTORY
argument is therefore not what is scanned (the walk is over the database);
it is the operation's safety scope. Naming a root that exists is the
evidence that the filesystem its rows describe is actually mounted and
live, and it confines the reconciliation to that subtree \(em so a database
that mirrors several drives can be pruned one drive at a time while the
others sit unplugged. The database itself must already exist; it is
never created by this option. A directory deleted from the filesystem but
moved elsewhere is pruned at its old location like any other deletion; files
deleted from a directory that still exists are not noticed. Combined with
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
previews what would be pruned without deleting anything. With
.BR \-\-progress ,
a live
.IR nnn / mmm
counter reports directories checked against the total known at the start.
Requires
.BR \-\-database .
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-sum ", " \-\-import ", " \-\-locate ", " \-\-verify ", " \-\-remove ", " \-\-force ", " \-\-prescan ", " \-\-db\-prescan ", " \-\-exclude ", or " \-\-no\-ignore
(the last two govern filesystem traversal, and this option walks the
database, not the filesystem). See
.B EXIT STATUS
below.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ignore
Disregard all
.B @sumtag\-ignore
marker files for this run, processing every directory regardless of markers.
This option governs markers only; it has no effect on
.BR \-\-exclude .
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " PATTERN"
Skip any file or directory whose name matches the shell glob
.IR PATTERN .
Matching is against the basename only, case-sensitively; a matching directory
is pruned along with its entire subtree, exactly as an
.B @sumtag\-ignore
marker would prune it. May be repeated; a name matching any given pattern is
excluded. The exclusion applies in every mode, is not overridden by
.BR \-\-force ,
and is unaffected by
.BR \-\-no\-ignore ,
which governs markers only. A
.I DIRECTORY
named on the command line whose own name matches a pattern is skipped, with a
warning printed to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH IGNORE MARKERS
A directory containing a file named
.B @sumtag\-ignore
is exempted along with its entire subtree: sumtag does not descend into it, and
nothing beneath it is read, hashed, stamped, or mirrored to a database. The
marker file itself is never hashed. Only the file's presence matters; its
contents are ignored and reserved for future use. The exemption applies in
every mode and takes precedence over
.BR \-\-force ;
use
.B \-\-no\-ignore
to override it. A marker found at a directory named directly on the command
line is honoured, with a warning printed to standard error.
.PP
For per-run exclusion from the command line, without placing a marker file,
see
.BR \-\-exclude .
.SH RUN SUMMARY
Every run ends with a brief summary block on standard output (suppressed by
.BR \-q ):
the number of files the mode processed and their cumulative size (headlined
.BR hashed ,
.BR "would hash" ,
.BR imported ,
.BR verified ", "
.BR removed ", or"
.B pruned
to match the mode;
.B pruned
counts directories and file rows, followed by a
.B checked
line with the total directories examined), any nonzero
.BR skipped ,
.BR errors ,
.BR CORRUPT ,
.BR stale ", or"
.B unverifiable
counts, the database in use (when
.B \-\-database
was given), and the scanned directories. Byte figures honour
.BR \-\-si .
.PP
Interrupting a run with Ctrl-C prints the same summary, prefixed by an
.B interrupted
line, instead of a Python traceback; the counters cover only files whose work
had already completed. The process then exits
.B 130
(128 + SIGINT, the shell convention).
.SH EXIT STATUS
Without
.BR \-\-verify " or " \-\-prune\-dirs ,
sumtag exits
.B 0
on success and non-zero if an error prevented completion.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-verify :
.TP
.B 0
all checked files verified intact.
.TP
.B 1
one or more checksum mismatches (corruption) were found.
.TP
.B 2
unreadable files or other errors prevented a complete check.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-prune\-dirs ,
the same scheme, applied to staleness:
.B 0
means nothing was stale (the database already matches the filesystem),
.B 1
means stale directories were found and pruned (or would be pruned, under
.BR \-\-dry\-run ),
and
.B 2
means errors prevented a complete check (including a missing scan root or
database).
.PP
In any mode, a run interrupted by Ctrl-C exits
.B 130
(see RUN SUMMARY).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B user.sumtag
Extended attribute holding each file's checksum and metadata as UTF-8 JSON.
.TP
.B @sumtag\-ignore
Marker file that exempts its directory and subtree (see IGNORE MARKERS).
.SH EXAMPLES
Stamp the current directory:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-sum .
.EE
.PP
Stamp two archives:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-sum /data /backup
.EE
.PP
Preview without writing anything:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-sum \-n /data
.EE
.PP
Stamp and also mirror into a SQLite database:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-database /var/lib/sumtag.sqlite \-\-sum /data
.EE
.PP
Import existing metadata into a database without re-reading files:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-database /var/lib/sumtag.sqlite \-\-import /data
.EE
.PP
Take a filesystem inventory pass, capturing stat metadata alongside any
existing checksums:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-database /var/lib/sumtag.sqlite \-\-locate /data
.EE
.PP
Verify an archive against its stored checksums:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-verify /backup
.EE
.PP
Resume an interrupted database run without repeating its hour-long counting
walk, reusing the totals the interrupted run's \-\-prescan stored:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-sum \-\-database /var/lib/sumtag.sqlite \-\-db\-prescan /data
.EE
.PP
Stamp an archive, skipping DVD rip payloads:
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-sum \-\-exclude '*.vob' \-\-exclude VIDEO_TS /data
.EE
.PP
After deleting directories from an archive, drop their stale database rows
(previewing first):
.PP
.EX
sumtag \-\-prune\-dirs \-n \-\-database /var/lib/sumtag.sqlite /data
sumtag \-\-prune\-dirs \-\-database /var/lib/sumtag.sqlite /data
.EE
.SH NOTES
Timestamps are recorded in UTC with microsecond precision. Mtime comparisons
truncate both the stored and live values to the same precision, so the
comparison is symmetric.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xattr (1),
.BR getfattr (1),
.BR setfattr (1),
.BR cksum (1)
.SH AUTHOR
The sumtag authors.
